\subsection{Symbolic Confirmation Bias}
\label{sec:confirmation-bias}

Self-extracted constraints systematically confirm the model's majority answer regardless of correctness. On Mistral-7B FOLIO ($n = 204$), when SICA errs, 83.3\% of extracted constraints support the incorrect majority answer---a bias ratio of $4.97\times$ (Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-bias}). Consequently, 94\% of SICA errors overlap with SC errors. The MAX-SAT solver rejects only 3.4\% of constraints on average, confirming degeneration (Eq.~\ref{eq:degenerate}).

The causal chain is: the model generates an answer, then extracts symbolic constraints consistent with it. Constraints use trace-specific variable names (56.4\% zero cross-answer variable overlap), so MAX-SAT retains nearly all and aggregation reduces to weighted majority voting. Confirmation bias generalizes across families: $4.97\times$ (Mistral), $3.93\times$ (Qwen-14B), and $2.88\times$ (LLaMA), all exceeding $1.0$ (Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-bias}). Bias intensifies along reasoning steps: late-step constraints (step $\geq 7$) exhibit 27.2\% conclusion leakage vs.\ 22.0\% for early steps (Fisher $p = 3 \times 10^{-6}$); SICA restricted to late-only constraints degrades by $-10.3$\,pp (Appendix~\ref{app:error-categorization}).


\subsection{Process-Level Independence}
\label{sec:process-kappa}

Answer-level $\kappa$ may underestimate the independence bottleneck: two traces can follow nearly identical reasoning yet reach different final answers, causing $\kappa$ to treat them as disagreeing. We define process similarity as the mean pairwise cosine similarity of TF-IDF trace representations:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:process-sim}
\text{ProcessSim}(q) = \frac{2}{K(K{-}1)} \sum_{i < j} \cos(\mathbf{v}_i^q, \mathbf{v}_j^q),
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{v}_i^q$ is the TF-IDF vector of trace $t_i$ for question $q$. On Mistral-7B FOLIO ($n = 204$), mean ProcessSim is $0.69$, far exceeding answer-level $\kappa = 0.41$. The process--answer correlation is model-strength dependent: $r = -0.06$ ($p = 0.36$) on Mistral-7B, where answer diversity does not reflect reasoning diversity (Appendix Figure~\ref{fig:process-kappa}), rising to $r = +0.24$ ($p < 0.001$) on Qwen2.5-14B. This pattern generalizes across four families (Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-process}): the cosine--$\kappa$ gap narrows from $0.23$ (Mistral) to $0.02$ (Qwen-14B), meaning answer-level $\kappa$ already captures the bottleneck for strong models, while process-$\kappa$ reveals hidden homogeneity in weaker models where answer diversity masks process convergence. In all families, interventions targeting answer diversity (temperature scaling, diverse prompting) cannot alter the underlying reasoning paths.


\subsection{Supporting Diagnostics}
\label{sec:diagnostics}

Further diagnostics---constraint ablation, discriminativeness, entropy decomposition, and hyperparameter sensitivity---confirm the mechanism (Appendices~\ref{app:extraction}--\ref{app:entropy}).


\subsection{Remediation Analysis}
\label{sec:remediation}
\label{sec:interventions}

Twenty-five-plus remediation strategies all produce neutral or negative results. At the prompt level, nine strategies fail: contrastive prompting ($\Delta = -1.1$\,pp), cross-model extraction ($\pm 1$\,pp), multi-temperature ($\Delta = 0.00$\,pp; Appendix Table~\ref{tab:remediation}). At the method level, ICG ($\Delta \approx 0$), Contrastive ($-9.31$\,pp, $p < 0.01$), and Debate ($-18.63$/$-23.00$\,pp, $p < 0.001$) all fail (Table~\ref{tab:method_interventions}). Fifteen further experiments produce neutral-to-negative results (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:additional_interventions}).

\paragraph{Independence-accuracy trade-off.} SC, Diverse-Prompt, and ICG cluster at $\kappa \approx 0.40$--$0.47$; Debate reaches $\kappa = 0.15$ but collapses accuracy by $-18.6$\,pp. All families cluster above $\kappa = 0.40$, confirming a same-model ceiling: methods that increase independence destroy accuracy, and vice versa (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:kappa_generalization}; Figure~\ref{fig:interventions-summary}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\columnwidth]{figures/fig_app_interventions_summary.pdf}
\caption{Summary of 22+ intervention strategies grouped by category. All produce neutral-to-negative accuracy changes relative to Self-Consistency. The grey band indicates $\pm 1$\,pp noise zone.}
\label{fig:interventions-summary}
\end{figure}

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related-work}

\paragraph{Self-Consistency and extensions.}
SC selects the most frequent answer across chain-of-thought traces \citep{wang2023sc}. Extensions include universal SC for free-form generation \citep{chen2024usc}, confidence weighting \citep{taubenfeld2025cisc}, diverse prompting with step-aware verification \citep{li2023diverse}, program-based vote filtering \citep{toh2024notallvotes}, and reasoning-aware filtering \citep{wan2024rasc, kim2026reasc}. \citet{loo2025reevaluating} show that SC gains plateau early. Our Condorcet analysis explains this: $\kappa = 0.21$--$0.82$ compresses $K_{\text{eff}}$ to 1.20--3.62 for $K = 12$ (\S\ref{sec:effective-k}).

\paragraph{Neuro-symbolic reasoning.}
Logic-LM \citep{pan2023logiclm} and LINC \citep{olausson2023linc} translate problems into formal logic for external provers; LINC documents ${\sim}$40\% translation errors. Recent extensions---CLOVER \citep{ryu2025clover}, DyLoS \citep{xu2026dylos}, Aristotle \citep{li2025aristotle}, FoVer \citep{wang2025fover}, VERGE \citep{singh2026verge}, LogicAgent \citep{luo2025logicagent}---add compositional translation, solver selection, decomposition, trained verifiers, and formal refinement, each introducing capabilities beyond the reasoning model. \citet{amonkar2025realitycheck} show that LLM formalization degrades with complexity; \citet{chan2026position} argue that logical soundness is not a reliable criterion for neurosymbolic verification with LLMs. Our oracle controls confirm the mechanism is sound ($+29.90$/$+14.22$\,pp FOLIO, $+11.50$\,pp ProofWriter); the failure localizes to self-extraction quality (\S\ref{sec:oracle}).

\paragraph{Test-time compute and verification.}
Scaling test-time computation through additional traces \citep{snell2025scaling} faces the same correlation challenge: same-model attempts collapse into correlated solutions. Reasoning models (o1, DeepSeek-R1 \citep{guo2025deepseekr1}, QwQ \citep{qwen2024qwq}) extend thinking time but remain subject to the independence bottleneck: QwQ-32B produces $\kappa = 0.91$ and $C_0 = 2.6\%$, suggesting that longer reasoning chains intensify homogenization rather than alleviating it. Trained verifiers offer an alternative \citep{cobbe2021gsm8k, lightman2024prm, wang2024mathshepherd}; multi-agent debate refines answers through discussion \citep{liang2023encouraging, du2023improving}. Answer-level self-verification \citep{weng2023selfverification} and iterative self-refinement \citep{madaan2023selfrefine} report gains using natural-language feedback, but neither employs formal symbolic verification. A natural hypothesis is that deterministic formal verification---where a Z3 solver provides logically guaranteed judgments---might bypass natural-language self-correction limitations by introducing a non-neural reasoning channel. We test this systematically and find the bottleneck shifts from the verifier to the extractor: self-extracted constraints inherit the model's bias regardless of verification formalism (\S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}). More broadly, self-correction without external feedback degrades performance \citep{huang2024selfcorrect, stechly2025selfverification}; our quantitative diagnostics (BR, Eff-$K$, process-$\kappa$) provide reusable tools for predicting when any trace-aggregation method will fail.

\paragraph{CJT and ensemble diversity.}
CJT \citep{condorcet1785} guarantees convergence under independent voting; \citet{ladha1992correlated} prove correlated voters reduce effective ensemble size; \citet{lefort2024condorcet_llm} apply CJT to LLM classification. We extend this by quantifying \emph{why} self-correction fails---same-model traces are correlated voters ($K_{\text{eff}}$ collapses)---and showing this persists even under formal verification (Z3-checked symbolic constraints) and at the process level (\S\ref{sec:process-kappa}).

\paragraph{Positioning.}
Prior self-correction studies \citep{huang2024selfcorrect, stechly2025selfverification} establish qualitative negative results; we contribute reusable quantitative diagnostics that characterize the failure mode. Effective-$K$ (Eq.~\ref{eq:effk}) measures how much independent information $K$ traces actually carry. The bias ratio ($\text{BR}$; Eq.~\ref{eq:mirage}) quantifies the degree to which self-extracted constraints post-hoc rationalize the model's answer rather than independently verify it, connecting symbolic extraction to CoT faithfulness concerns \citep{turpin2024language}. Conclusion leakage rate ($C_D$) isolates answer-dependent constraint contamination at the step level. Together, these diagnostics show that formal Z3-checked verification inherits the same bias ($\text{BR} = 4.97\times$)---a finding invisible to prior work that examines only natural-language self-correction. The cross-architecture escape (\S\ref{sec:cross-arch}) yields a capability gap corollary: external verification helps only when the verifier's standalone accuracy exceeds the generator's SC baseline, providing a practical decision criterion absent from existing frameworks.

