\section{Cross-Architecture Proof-of-Concept}
\label{sec:solutions}
\label{sec:cross-arch}

\paragraph{Method.}
We test this with DeBERTa-large \citep{he2021deberta} (406M, encoder-only), fine-tuned on MultiNLI \citep{williams2018mnli}, classifying (premise, conclusion) pairs into \textsc{entailment}, \textsc{contradiction}, or \textsc{neutral}. In combo mode, the NLI verdict is integrated with SC's $K$ votes via weight $w$: each NLI-supporting vote counts $w$ times. We evaluate on Mistral-7B FOLIO ($n = 204$) and ProofWriter depth-5 ($n = 600$), because NLI classification requires (premise, conclusion) pairs that these datasets naturally provide; multiple-choice datasets (LogiQA) lack this structure.

\paragraph{Main result.}
DeBERTa NLI alone achieves 49.51\% on FOLIO (below SC's 55.88\%) and 55.00\% on ProofWriter (above SC's 39.33\%). On FOLIO ($n = 204$), combo mode yields $+1.96$\,pp at $w{=}1$ and $+2.94$\,pp at $w{=}3$ (both n.s.). On ProofWriter ($n = 600$), $w{=}3$ achieves $+3.50$\,pp ($p = 0.013$) and $w{=}5$ reaches $+10.67$\,pp ($p < 0.001$)---while same-model SICA \emph{degrades} ProofWriter ($-2.83$\,pp, $p = 0.014$; Table~\ref{tab:main_results}), illustrating that corrective signal must originate outside the model's distribution (\S\ref{sec:mirage}). We report $w{=}3$ as primary; higher weights risk overfitting. After Benjamini--Hochberg correction ($m{=}6$), ProofWriter $w{=}3$ remains significant (adjusted $p = 0.038$) and $w{=}5$ ($p < 0.001$); FOLIO comparisons are non-significant. Per-label caveat: DeBERTa over-predicts \textsc{False} on ProofWriter (67.8\%) and performs poorly on \textsc{Unknown} (3.5\% accuracy), as NLI's ``neutral'' fails to capture logical unknowability.

\paragraph{Why it works.}
The 406M encoder-only DeBERTa and 7B decoder-only Mistral share no architecture, training data, or inference mechanism, satisfying the independence condition that same-model sampling cannot provide (\S\ref{sec:mirage}). This produces per-label complementarity: NLI excels on \textsc{False} problems (71.43\% vs.\ SC's 38.10\%) where the LLM is weakest, while SC dominates \textsc{Unknown} (68.12\% vs.\ 18.84\%). All three NLI architectures tested---DeBERTa, RoBERTa, and BART (all MNLI-trained)---produce significant improvements on ProofWriter at $w \geq 3$ (Appendix~\ref{app:multi-nli}), confirming the effect is not verifier-specific.

\paragraph{Capability gap hypothesis.}
Cross-architecture verification succeeds when the verifier's standalone accuracy exceeds the generator's SC baseline. We evaluate across two generator families: on ProofWriter, DeBERTa standalone (55.00\%) exceeds Mistral-7B SC (39.33\%), enabling $+10.67$\,pp at $w{=}5$ ($p < 0.001$). On Qwen3-14B FOLIO, where SC (86.27\%) far exceeds DeBERTa (49.51\%), the combo yields $\Delta \leq +0.49$\,pp (all $p = 1.0$). Aggregation gain shrinks with model capability: $+8.01$\,pp (Mistral-7B) to $+0.86$\,pp (Qwen3-14B thinking mode; Appendix~\ref{app:single-trace}). Across all conditions at $w{=}3$, combo gain tracks the capability gap (Figure~\ref{fig:capability-gap}): meaningful improvement emerges only when the verifier's standalone accuracy approaches or exceeds the generator's SC baseline.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.82\columnwidth]{figures/capability_gap_scatter}
\vspace{-3mm}
\caption{Combo gain vs.\ capability gap (verifier standalone $-$ generator SC) at $w{=}3$. Meaningful gain emerges when the gap is positive (verifier exceeds generator).}
\label{fig:capability-gap}
\vspace{-6mm}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Cross-generator results.}
Table~\ref{tab:cross-generator-nli} extends the NLI combo across two generators and four verifiers. Three of four Mistral-7B verifiers produce significant gains ($p < 0.001$), while neither Qwen3-14B condition reaches significance. The pattern is consistent: NLI escape is strongest when the generator is weak (SC $= 39.33\%$) and the verifier's standalone accuracy approaches or exceeds the generator's baseline. When the generator is strong (SC $= 85.00\%$), even architecturally independent verifiers cannot contribute corrective signal, confirming that the capability gap is the binding constraint rather than architectural diversity alone.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-generator NLI combo on ProofWriter depth-5 ($n{=}600$, $w{=}5$). Stand.\ = NLI standalone accuracy (\%). \textbf{Bold $\Delta$}: $p < 0.05$.}
\label{tab:cross-generator-nli}
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llrrrr@{}}
\toprule
Generator & NLI Verifier & SC (\%) & Stand.\ (\%) & $\Delta$ (pp) & $p$ \\
\midrule
Mistral-7B & DeBERTa-base & 39.33 & 55.00 & $\mathbf{+10.67}$ & ${<}.001$ \\
Mistral-7B & BART-large & 39.33 & 46.20 & $\mathbf{+7.17}$ & ${<}.001$ \\
Mistral-7B & RoBERTa-large & 39.33 & 37.70 & $\mathbf{+6.50}$ & ${<}.001$ \\
Mistral-7B & DeBERTa-v3-lg & 39.33 & 45.17 & $+4.83$ & .110 \\
\addlinespace
Qwen3-14B & DeBERTa-base & 85.00 & 36.17 & $+0.33$ & .830 \\
Qwen3-14B & DeBERTa-v3-lg & 85.00 & 45.17 & $-0.67$ & .800 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

